solnix: usbecm -- report link DOWN when the adapter is unplugged (and UP on reconnect), so traffic moves to the
other interface.

SYMPTOM (ThinkPad X1 Extreme Gen5 "lixi", Belkin USB-C LAN adapter on usbecm1 next to the AX211 wifi on iwx0, both
DHCP on 192.168.1.0/24, maintainer 2026-10-26): unplugging the adapter stopped the network. Rebooting with it
out came up on wifi and worked.

MECHANISM (code reading; measurement pending): usbecm_disconnect_event_cb sets USB_DEV_DISCONNECTED and closes the
pipes, but never calls mac_link_update(). The MAC layer keeps usbecm1's link UP, so IP never clears IFF_RUNNING
on it (ip.c DL_NOTE_LINK_DOWN is what clears PHYI_RUNNING). dhcpagent removes an interface's routes only when
IFF_RUNNING goes away (agent.c process_link_up_down -> pif_status -> remove_routes). So usbecm1's default route,
its 192.168.1.0/24 route and its address all stay. ire_round_robin spreads flows over the two equal default and
subnet routes, and some of them keep going to the vanished adapter. The same effect was measured in the other
direction on lixi (wifi disconnected while the wired link stayed up: 68% loss for the minute wifi was gone).

THE CHANGE (usbecm.c only):
- usbecm_link_set(): report a change once, with es_linkstate tracking it and mac_link_update called with no lock
  held, as m_start, m_stop and the CDC NETWORK_CONNECTION notification already do.
- disconnect: after the pipes are closed, link DOWN. usbgem drivers (axf/urf/udmf) get the same through
  usbgem_mac_stop on disconnect.
- reconnect/resume (usbecm_restore_device_state): once the pipes and rx are restarted and the MAC was started,
  link UP, as m_start reports it. ECM gives the host no way to ask for the link state, and a device that has lost
  carrier says so through its notification pipe.
RUNG: compiles in the gate (the build is the check). NOT yet measured on lixi: the plug/unplug test with the route
table and pings is next.

--- a/usr/src/uts/common/io/usb/clients/usbecm/usbecm.c	2026-10-26 21:53:58.310593034 -0400
+++ b/usr/src/uts/common/io/usb/clients/usbecm/usbecm.c	2026-10-26 21:56:02.050833785 -0400
@@ -1720,6 +1720,36 @@
 }
 
 /*
+ * Report a link state change once: mac_link_update with no lock held, and
+ * es_linkstate tracking it, as usbecm_m_start/m_stop and the CDC
+ * NETWORK_CONNECTION notification already do.
+ */
+static void
+usbecm_link_set(usbecm_state_t *ecmp, link_state_t ls)
+{
+	mutex_enter(&ecmp->ecm_mutex);
+	if (ecmp->ecm_stat.es_linkstate == ls) {
+		mutex_exit(&ecmp->ecm_mutex);
+		return;
+	}
+	ecmp->ecm_stat.es_linkstate = ls;
+	mutex_exit(&ecmp->ecm_mutex);
+	mac_link_update(ecmp->ecm_mh, ls);
+}
+
+static void
+usbecm_link_down(usbecm_state_t *ecmp)
+{
+	usbecm_link_set(ecmp, LINK_STATE_DOWN);
+}
+
+static void
+usbecm_link_up(usbecm_state_t *ecmp)
+{
+	usbecm_link_set(ecmp, LINK_STATE_UP);
+}
+
+/*
  * usbecm_restore_device_state:
  *	restore device state after CPR resume or reconnect
  */
@@ -1727,6 +1757,7 @@
 usbecm_restore_device_state(usbecm_state_t *ecmp)
 {
 	int	state;
+	boolean_t	started;
 
 	USB_DPRINTF_L4(PRINT_MASK_EVENTS, ecmp->ecm_lh,
 	    "usbecm_restore_device_state: ");
@@ -1784,8 +1815,17 @@
 	 */
 	mutex_enter(&ecmp->ecm_mutex);
 	state = ecmp->ecm_dev_state = USB_DEV_ONLINE;
+	started = (ecmp->ecm_mac_state == USBECM_MAC_STARTED);
 	mutex_exit(&ecmp->ecm_mutex);
 
+	/*
+	 * Reconnected with the MAC started: the link is usable again, as
+	 * usbecm_m_start reports it (ECM gives the host no way to ask; a device
+	 * that has lost carrier will say so through its notification pipe).
+	 */
+	if (started)
+		usbecm_link_up(ecmp);
+
 	return (state);
 }
 
@@ -1849,6 +1889,15 @@
 
 	usb_release_access(ecmp->ecm_ser_acc);
 
+	/*
+	 * The device is gone, so the link is down. Without this the MAC layer
+	 * keeps the link UP, IP keeps IFF_RUNNING, and dhcpagent keeps this
+	 * interface's routes, so traffic goes on being sent to a cable that is
+	 * no longer there instead of moving to another interface. usbgem's
+	 * disconnect path (usbgem_mac_stop) and m_stop here do the same.
+	 */
+	usbecm_link_down(ecmp);
+
 	USB_DPRINTF_L4(PRINT_MASK_EVENTS, ecmp->ecm_lh,
 	    "usbecm_disconnect_event_cb: End");
 
